{"type":"tool_execution_update","toolCallId":"toolu_01ErCSoXLkjaKCRpTSVusuqF","toolName":"bash","args":{"command":"cd /app && sed -n 1,80p .ki-base/decisions/0001-terminal-segments.md; echo ---; cat rich/measure.py | head -60; echo ---; grep -rn \"__all__\" tests | head; echo ---; grep -n \"def cell_len\" -A3 rich/cells.py; echo ---; sed -n 1,40p rich/rule.py"},"partialResult":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# ADR-0001: Terminal Segments & Atomic Decomposition\n\n- **Status:** Accepted\n- **Date:** 2026-11-08\n- **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers\n\n## Context\n\nTerminal emulators operate on streams of characters interspersed with ANSI escape codes for styling and cursor movement. Without a unified primitive abstraction, high-level components (tables, syntax, markdown) would have to manually track escape codes, calculate visual cell widths, and handle cross-platform line breaks, leading to exponential complexity and bugs.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Atomic Segment Primitive:**  \n   Every visual element in Rich must ultimately reduce to a stream of `Segment` instances:\n   ```python\n   class Segment(NamedTuple):\n       text: str\n       style: Optional[Style] = None\n       control: Optional[Sequence[ControlCode]] = None\n   ```\n2. **Strict Invariants:**\n   - A `Segment` text piece must not contain unescaped newline characters `\\n` or `\\r` unless processed by `Segment.split_lines()` or `Segment.split_and_crop_lines()`.\n   - Visual measurement is derived strictly from `cell_len(segment.text)` using East Asian Width Unicode tables, never raw `len(text)`.\n   - Control sequences (cursor up, color palette queries) are encapsulated in the optional `control` field and take zero visual cell length.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- All rendering operations are stateless stream-processing pipelines.\n- Layout algorithms only need to measure and slice `Segment` collections.\n- Clean separation between logical layout calculation and physical terminal escape emission.\n"}],"details":{}}}
{"type":"tool_execution_update","toolCallId":"toolu_01ErCSoXLkjaKCRpTSVusuqF","toolName":"bash","args":{"command":"cd /app && sed -n 1,80p .ki-base/decisions/0001-terminal-segments.md; echo ---; cat rich/measure.py | head -60; echo ---; grep -rn \"__all__\" tests | head; echo ---; grep -n \"def cell_len\" -A3 rich/cells.py; echo ---; sed -n 1,40p rich/rule.py"},"partialResult":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# ADR-0001: Terminal Segments & Atomic Decomposition\n\n- **Status:** Accepted\n- **Date:** 2026-11-08\n- **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers\n\n## Context\n\nTerminal emulators operate on streams of characters interspersed with ANSI escape codes for styling and cursor movement. Without a unified primitive abstraction, high-level components (tables, syntax, markdown) would have to manually track escape codes, calculate visual cell widths, and handle cross-platform line breaks, leading to exponential complexity and bugs.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Atomic Segment Primitive:**  \n   Every visual element in Rich must ultimately reduce to a stream of `Segment` instances:\n   ```python\n   class Segment(NamedTuple):\n       text: str\n       style: Optional[Style] = None\n       control: Optional[Sequence[ControlCode]] = None\n   ```\n2. **Strict Invariants:**\n   - A `Segment` text piece must not contain unescaped newline characters `\\n` or `\\r` unless processed by `Segment.split_lines()` or `Segment.split_and_crop_lines()`.\n   - Visual measurement is derived strictly from `cell_len(segment.text)` using East Asian Width Unicode tables, never raw `len(text)`.\n   - Control sequences (cursor up, color palette queries) are encapsulated in the optional `control` field and take zero visual cell length.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- All rendering operations are stateless stream-processing pipelines.\n- Layout algorithms only need to measure and slice `Segment` collections.\n- Clean separation between logical layout calculation and physical terminal escape emission.\n---\nfrom operator import itemgetter\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Callable, NamedTuple, Optional, Sequence\n\nfrom . import errors\nfrom .protocol import is_renderable, rich_cast\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from .console import Console, ConsoleOptions, RenderableType\n\n\nclass Measurement(NamedTuple):\n    \"\"\"Stores the minimum and maximum widths (in characters) required to render an object.\"\"\"\n\n    minimum: int\n    \"\"\"Minimum number of cells required to render.\"\"\"\n    maximum: int\n    \"\"\"Maximum number of cells required to render.\"\"\"\n\n    @property\n    def span(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Get difference between maximum and minimum.\"\"\"\n        return self.maximum - self.minimum\n\n    def normalize(self) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get measurement that ensures that minimum <= maximum and minimum >= 0\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: A normalized measurement.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        minimum = min(max(0, minimum), maximum)\n        return Measurement(max(0, minimum), max(0, max(minimum, maximum)))\n\n    def with_maximum(self, width: int) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get a RenderableWith where the widths are <= width.\n\n        Args:\n            width (int): Maximum desired width.\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: New Measurement object.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        return Measurement(min(minimum, width), min(maximum, width))\n\n    def with_minimum(self, width: int) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get a RenderableWith where the widths are >= width.\n\n        Args:\n            width (int): Minimum desired width.\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: New Measurement object.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        width = max(0, width)\n        return Measurement(max(minimum, width), max(maximum, width))\n\n    def clamp(\n        self, min_width: Optional[int] = None, max_width: Optional[int] = None\n---\n"}],"details":{}}}
{"type":"tool_execution_update","toolCallId":"toolu_01ErCSoXLkjaKCRpTSVusuqF","toolName":"bash","args":{"command":"cd /app && sed -n 1,80p .ki-base/decisions/0001-terminal-segments.md; echo ---; cat rich/measure.py | head -60; echo ---; grep -rn \"__all__\" tests | head; echo ---; grep -n \"def cell_len\" -A3 rich/cells.py; echo ---; sed -n 1,40p rich/rule.py"},"partialResult":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# ADR-0001: Terminal Segments & Atomic Decomposition\n\n- **Status:** Accepted\n- **Date:** 2026-11-08\n- **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers\n\n## Context\n\nTerminal emulators operate on streams of characters interspersed with ANSI escape codes for styling and cursor movement. Without a unified primitive abstraction, high-level components (tables, syntax, markdown) would have to manually track escape codes, calculate visual cell widths, and handle cross-platform line breaks, leading to exponential complexity and bugs.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Atomic Segment Primitive:**  \n   Every visual element in Rich must ultimately reduce to a stream of `Segment` instances:\n   ```python\n   class Segment(NamedTuple):\n       text: str\n       style: Optional[Style] = None\n       control: Optional[Sequence[ControlCode]] = None\n   ```\n2. **Strict Invariants:**\n   - A `Segment` text piece must not contain unescaped newline characters `\\n` or `\\r` unless processed by `Segment.split_lines()` or `Segment.split_and_crop_lines()`.\n   - Visual measurement is derived strictly from `cell_len(segment.text)` using East Asian Width Unicode tables, never raw `len(text)`.\n   - Control sequences (cursor up, color palette queries) are encapsulated in the optional `control` field and take zero visual cell length.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- All rendering operations are stateless stream-processing pipelines.\n- Layout algorithms only need to measure and slice `Segment` collections.\n- Clean separation between logical layout calculation and physical terminal escape emission.\n---\nfrom operator import itemgetter\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Callable, NamedTuple, Optional, Sequence\n\nfrom . import errors\nfrom .protocol import is_renderable, rich_cast\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from .console import Console, ConsoleOptions, RenderableType\n\n\nclass Measurement(NamedTuple):\n    \"\"\"Stores the minimum and maximum widths (in characters) required to render an object.\"\"\"\n\n    minimum: int\n    \"\"\"Minimum number of cells required to render.\"\"\"\n    maximum: int\n    \"\"\"Maximum number of cells required to render.\"\"\"\n\n    @property\n    def span(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Get difference between maximum and minimum.\"\"\"\n        return self.maximum - self.minimum\n\n    def normalize(self) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get measurement that ensures that minimum <= maximum and minimum >= 0\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: A normalized measurement.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        minimum = min(max(0, minimum), maximum)\n        return Measurement(max(0, minimum), max(0, max(minimum, maximum)))\n\n    def with_maximum(self, width: int) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get a RenderableWith where the widths are <= width.\n\n        Args:\n            width (int): Maximum desired width.\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: New Measurement object.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        return Measurement(min(minimum, width), min(maximum, width))\n\n    def with_minimum(self, width: int) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get a RenderableWith where the widths are >= width.\n\n        Args:\n            width (int): Minimum desired width.\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: New Measurement object.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        width = max(0, width)\n        return Measurement(max(minimum, width), max(maximum, width))\n\n    def clamp(\n        self, min_width: Optional[int] = None, max_width: Optional[int] = None\n---\n---\n98:def cell_len(text: str, unicode_version: str = \"auto\") -> int:\n99-    \"\"\"Get the cell length of a string (length as it appears in the terminal).\n100-\n101-    Args:\n---\nfrom typing import Union\n\nfrom .align import AlignMethod\nfrom .cells import cell_len, set_cell_size\nfrom .console import Console, ConsoleOptions, RenderResult\nfrom .jupyter import JupyterMixin\nfrom .measure import Measurement\nfrom .style import Style\nfrom .text import Text\n\n\nclass Rule(JupyterMixin):\n    \"\"\"A console renderable to draw a horizontal rule (line).\n\n    Args:\n        title (Union[str, Text], optional): Text to render in the rule. Defaults to \"\".\n        characters (str, optional): Character(s) used to draw the line. Defaults to \"─\".\n        style (StyleType, optional): Style of Rule. Defaults to \"rule.line\".\n        end (str, optional): Character at end of Rule. defaults to \"\\\\\\\\n\"\n        align (str, optional): How to align the title, one of \"left\", \"center\", or \"right\". Defaults to \"center\".\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        title: Union[str, Text] = \"\",\n        *,\n        characters: str = \"─\",\n        style: Union[str, Style] = \"rule.line\",\n        end: str = \"\\n\",\n        align: AlignMethod = \"center\",\n    ) -> None:\n        if cell_len(characters) < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"'characters' argument must have a cell width of at least 1\"\n            )\n        if align not in (\"left\", \"center\", \"right\"):\n            raise ValueError(\n                f'invalid value for align, expected \"left\", \"center\", \"right\" (not {align!r})'\n            )\n        self.title = title\n"}],"details":{}}}
{"type":"tool_execution_end","toolCallId":"toolu_01ErCSoXLkjaKCRpTSVusuqF","toolName":"bash","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# ADR-0001: Terminal Segments & Atomic Decomposition\n\n- **Status:** Accepted\n- **Date:** 2026-11-08\n- **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers\n\n## Context\n\nTerminal emulators operate on streams of characters interspersed with ANSI escape codes for styling and cursor movement. Without a unified primitive abstraction, high-level components (tables, syntax, markdown) would have to manually track escape codes, calculate visual cell widths, and handle cross-platform line breaks, leading to exponential complexity and bugs.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Atomic Segment Primitive:**  \n   Every visual element in Rich must ultimately reduce to a stream of `Segment` instances:\n   ```python\n   class Segment(NamedTuple):\n       text: str\n       style: Optional[Style] = None\n       control: Optional[Sequence[ControlCode]] = None\n   ```\n2. **Strict Invariants:**\n   - A `Segment` text piece must not contain unescaped newline characters `\\n` or `\\r` unless processed by `Segment.split_lines()` or `Segment.split_and_crop_lines()`.\n   - Visual measurement is derived strictly from `cell_len(segment.text)` using East Asian Width Unicode tables, never raw `len(text)`.\n   - Control sequences (cursor up, color palette queries) are encapsulated in the optional `control` field and take zero visual cell length.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- All rendering operations are stateless stream-processing pipelines.\n- Layout algorithms only need to measure and slice `Segment` collections.\n- Clean separation between logical layout calculation and physical terminal escape emission.\n---\nfrom operator import itemgetter\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Callable, NamedTuple, Optional, Sequence\n\nfrom . import errors\nfrom .protocol import is_renderable, rich_cast\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from .console import Console, ConsoleOptions, RenderableType\n\n\nclass Measurement(NamedTuple):\n    \"\"\"Stores the minimum and maximum widths (in characters) required to render an object.\"\"\"\n\n    minimum: int\n    \"\"\"Minimum number of cells required to render.\"\"\"\n    maximum: int\n    \"\"\"Maximum number of cells required to render.\"\"\"\n\n    @property\n    def span(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Get difference between maximum and minimum.\"\"\"\n        return self.maximum - self.minimum\n\n    def normalize(self) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get measurement that ensures that minimum <= maximum and minimum >= 0\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: A normalized measurement.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        minimum = min(max(0, minimum), maximum)\n        return Measurement(max(0, minimum), max(0, max(minimum, maximum)))\n\n    def with_maximum(self, width: int) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get a RenderableWith where the widths are <= width.\n\n        Args:\n            width (int): Maximum desired width.\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: New Measurement object.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        return Measurement(min(minimum, width), min(maximum, width))\n\n    def with_minimum(self, width: int) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get a RenderableWith where the widths are >= width.\n\n        Args:\n            width (int): Minimum desired width.\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: New Measurement object.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        width = max(0, width)\n        return Measurement(max(minimum, width), max(maximum, width))\n\n    def clamp(\n        self, min_width: Optional[int] = None, max_width: Optional[int] = None\n---\n---\n98:def cell_len(text: str, unicode_version: str = \"auto\") -> int:\n99-    \"\"\"Get the cell length of a string (length as it appears in the terminal).\n100-\n101-    Args:\n---\nfrom typing import Union\n\nfrom .align import AlignMethod\nfrom .cells import cell_len, set_cell_size\nfrom .console import Console, ConsoleOptions, RenderResult\nfrom .jupyter import JupyterMixin\nfrom .measure import Measurement\nfrom .style import Style\nfrom .text import Text\n\n\nclass Rule(JupyterMixin):\n    \"\"\"A console renderable to draw a horizontal rule (line).\n\n    Args:\n        title (Union[str, Text], optional): Text to render in the rule. Defaults to \"\".\n        characters (str, optional): Character(s) used to draw the line. Defaults to \"─\".\n        style (StyleType, optional): Style of Rule. Defaults to \"rule.line\".\n        end (str, optional): Character at end of Rule. defaults to \"\\\\\\\\n\"\n        align (str, optional): How to align the title, one of \"left\", \"center\", or \"right\". Defaults to \"center\".\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        title: Union[str, Text] = \"\",\n        *,\n        characters: str = \"─\",\n        style: Union[str, Style] = \"rule.line\",\n        end: str = \"\\n\",\n        align: AlignMethod = \"center\",\n    ) -> None:\n        if cell_len(characters) < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"'characters' argument must have a cell width of at least 1\"\n            )\n        if align not in (\"left\", \"center\", \"right\"):\n            raise ValueError(\n                f'invalid value for align, expected \"left\", \"center\", \"right\" (not {align!r})'\n            )\n        self.title = title\n"}],"structuredContent":{"output":"# ADR-0001: Terminal Segments & Atomic Decomposition\n\n- **Status:** Accepted\n- **Date:** 2026-11-08\n- **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers\n\n## Context\n\nTerminal emulators operate on streams of characters interspersed with ANSI escape codes for styling and cursor movement. Without a unified primitive abstraction, high-level components (tables, syntax, markdown) would have to manually track escape codes, calculate visual cell widths, and handle cross-platform line breaks, leading to exponential complexity and bugs.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Atomic Segment Primitive:**  \n   Every visual element in Rich must ultimately reduce to a stream of `Segment` instances:\n   ```python\n   class Segment(NamedTuple):\n       text: str\n       style: Optional[Style] = None\n       control: Optional[Sequence[ControlCode]] = None\n   ```\n2. **Strict Invariants:**\n   - A `Segment` text piece must not contain unescaped newline characters `\\n` or `\\r` unless processed by `Segment.split_lines()` or `Segment.split_and_crop_lines()`.\n   - Visual measurement is derived strictly from `cell_len(segment.text)` using East Asian Width Unicode tables, never raw `len(text)`.\n   - Control sequences (cursor up, color palette queries) are encapsulated in the optional `control` field and take zero visual cell length.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- All rendering operations are stateless stream-processing pipelines.\n- Layout algorithms only need to measure and slice `Segment` collections.\n- Clean separation between logical layout calculation and physical terminal escape emission.\n---\nfrom operator import itemgetter\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Callable, NamedTuple, Optional, Sequence\n\nfrom . import errors\nfrom .protocol import is_renderable, rich_cast\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from .console import Console, ConsoleOptions, RenderableType\n\n\nclass Measurement(NamedTuple):\n    \"\"\"Stores the minimum and maximum widths (in characters) required to render an object.\"\"\"\n\n    minimum: int\n    \"\"\"Minimum number of cells required to render.\"\"\"\n    maximum: int\n    \"\"\"Maximum number of cells required to render.\"\"\"\n\n    @property\n    def span(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Get difference between maximum and minimum.\"\"\"\n        return self.maximum - self.minimum\n\n    def normalize(self) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get measurement that ensures that minimum <= maximum and minimum >= 0\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: A normalized measurement.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        minimum = min(max(0, minimum), maximum)\n        return Measurement(max(0, minimum), max(0, max(minimum, maximum)))\n\n    def with_maximum(self, width: int) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get a RenderableWith where the widths are <= width.\n\n        Args:\n            width (int): Maximum desired width.\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: New Measurement object.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        return Measurement(min(minimum, width), min(maximum, width))\n\n    def with_minimum(self, width: int) -> \"Measurement\":\n        \"\"\"Get a RenderableWith where the widths are >= width.\n\n        Args:\n            width (int): Minimum desired width.\n\n        Returns:\n            Measurement: New Measurement object.\n        \"\"\"\n        minimum, maximum = self\n        width = max(0, width)\n        return Measurement(max(minimum, width), max(maximum, width))\n\n    def clamp(\n        self, min_width: Optional[int] = None, max_width: Optional[int] = None\n---\n---\n98:def cell_len(text: str, unicode_version: str = \"auto\") -> int:\n99-    \"\"\"Get the cell length of a string (length as it appears in the terminal).\n100-\n101-    Args:\n---\nfrom typing import Union\n\nfrom .align import AlignMethod\nfrom .cells import cell_len, set_cell_size\nfrom .console import Console, ConsoleOptions, RenderResult\nfrom .jupyter import JupyterMixin\nfrom .measure import Measurement\nfrom .style import Style\nfrom .text import Text\n\n\nclass Rule(JupyterMixin):\n    \"\"\"A console renderable to draw a horizontal rule (line).\n\n    Args:\n        title (Union[str, Text], optional): Text to render in the rule. Defaults to \"\".\n        characters (str, optional): Character(s) used to draw the line. Defaults to \"─\".\n        style (StyleType, optional): Style of Rule. Defaults to \"rule.line\".\n        end (str, optional): Character at end of Rule. defaults to \"\\\\\\\\n\"\n        align (str, optional): How to align the title, one of \"left\", \"center\", or \"right\". Defaults to \"center\".\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        title: Union[str, Text] = \"\",\n        *,\n        characters: str = \"─\",\n        style: Union[str, Style] = \"rule.line\",\n        end: str = \"\\n\",\n        align: AlignMethod = \"center\",\n    ) -> None:\n        if cell_len(characters) < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"'characters' argument must have a cell width of at least 1\"\n            )\n        if align not in (\"left\", \"center\", \"right\"):\n            raise ValueError(\n                f'invalid value for align, expected \"left\", \"center\", \"right\" (not {align!r})'\n            )\n        self.title = title\n","truncated":false,"exit_code":0,"wall_time_seconds":0.2}},"isError":false,"durationMs":205}
